Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic period drama; naturalistic faces, fabric, fog, and candlelight; one consistent live-action look for all cast and spirits.

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Fog clings to blackened brick and iron railings. A weathered sign reads SCROOGE AND MARLEY. Snow dusts the cobbles. EBENEZER SCROOGE, elderly, thin, pointed nose, blue thin lips, frosty rime on brows and wiry chin, black great-coat, walks alone; people part from him.

(SOUND: distant church bell, wheezing footsteps, wind in the fog)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

Scrooge does not look up. A blind man's dog tugs its owner into a doorway.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh! But he was a tight-fisted hand at the grind-stone, Scrooge! a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous, old sinner!

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A dismal office. Scrooge at a high desk over a tiny fire. Beyond, in a tank-like cell, BOB CRATCHIT, threadbare, white comforter, copies letters at a fire of one coal. Fog seeps at the keyhole.

(SOUND: scratch of quills, coal settling, fog-muffled street noise)

The door bursts open. FRED, young, ruddy, handsome, eyes sparkling, breath smoking, strides in glowing from the cold.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? Out upon merry Christmas!

Scrooge slams a ledger shut. Bob flinches, warms his hands at his candle.

SCROOGE
If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with Merry Christmas on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it. I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time.

Bob involuntarily applauds, then pokes his fire and kills the last spark.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Why did you get married?

FRED
Because I fell in love.

SCROOGE
Because you fell in love! Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you; why cannot we be friends? A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without anger. At the outer door he greets Bob warmly. Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers, hats off.

(SOUND: door latch, muted street carol far off)

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
They are. Still. I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid, from what you said at first, that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink, and means of warmth. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. It's not my business. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes work, pleased with himself.

EXT. COUNTING-HOUSE DOOR / STREET - DUSK

Piercing cold. A thin CAROL BOY stoops at the keyhole.

(SOUND: boy's thin voice starts a carol)

CAROL BOY
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

Scrooge seizes a ruler. The boy flees into the fog.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - NIGHT

Closing time. Bob snuffs his candle and puts on his hat.

(SOUND: clock chime, scrape of stool)

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises. Scrooge growls out. Bob, comforter dangling, runs off toward Camden Town.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - YARD - NIGHT

A gloomy pile up a dark yard. Fog and frost hang on the black gateway. Scrooge gropes to his door, key in hand. The large knocker becomes MARLEY'S FACE: livid, spectacles on ghostly forehead, hair stirred, eyes wide and motionless.

(SOUND: fog drip, distant bell, Scrooge's sharp intake of breath)

Scrooge stares. It is a knocker again. He turns the key, enters, lights a candle.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - HALL AND STAIRS - NIGHT

Scrooge checks behind the door — only screws and nuts. He bangs it shut. Echoes boom through the house.

(SOUND: door thunder, rolling echoes up the stairwell)

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He climbs the wide dark stairs, trimming his candle, half-seeing a hearse in the gloom. Darkness is cheap. He likes it.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - SITTING-ROOM - NIGHT

Sitting-room, bedroom beyond. Small fire, spoon and basin, little saucepan of gruel on the hob. Scrooge, in dressing-gown, slippers, nightcap, double-locks the door, sits close to the meagre fire. Dutch tiles around the grate. Marley's face seems to swim on every tile.

(SOUND: low fire hiss, house settling)

SCROOGE
Humbug!

He paces, sits again. A disused bell begins to swing — softly, then loud. Every bell in the house answers.

(SOUND: soft bell swing building to a clangour of all the house bells)

The bells cease. A clanking rises from below, as of a heavy chain dragged over casks. The cellar-door booms. The noise climbs the stairs, comes straight toward his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

Without pause the thing passes through the heavy door. The dying flame leaps up and falls. MARLEY'S GHOST stands before him: pigtail, waistcoat, tights and boots; chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, steel purses clasped about his middle; body transparent; folded kerchief about head and chin.

(SOUND: chain drag and clank, flame whoosh, death-cold silence)

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

SCROOGE
Do it, then.

The Ghost sits opposite the fireplace. Hair, skirts, and tassels stir as by hot vapour.

MARLEY'S GHOST
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. Scrooge clings to his chair. The Ghost removes the bandage; its lower jaw drops upon its breast. Scrooge falls to his knees.

(SOUND: appalling chain rattle, jaw clack, Scrooge's choked cry)

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

The Ghost wrings its shadowy hands and shakes the chain again.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will. Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge trembles, glances at the floor for his own chain, sees nothing.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it since. It is a ponderous chain!

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. In life my spirit never roved beyond the narrow limits of our money-changing hole; and weary journeys lie before me!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

The Ghost flings the chain heavily upon the ground.

(SOUND: chain crash on floorboards)

MARLEY'S GHOST
Hear me! My time is nearly gone. I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer.

SCROOGE
You were always a good friend to me. Thank'ee!

MARLEY'S GHOST
You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds the wrapper round its head. Teeth smart together. It walks backward; the window rises by itself. At the sill it beckons, then warns Scrooge back. Confused wailings fill the air. The Ghost joins the dirge and floats out into the bleak night.

(SOUND: window sash rising, lamentation on the wind, chain fading)

Scrooge looks out. The air is filled with fettered phantoms moaning in restless haste; one old ghost with an iron safe at its ankle cries over a woman and infant on a door-step. They fade into mist. Scrooge closes the window, checks the double-locked door, tries to say "Humbug," fails, and falls asleep upon the bed without undressing.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Deep dark. Church chimes strike the quarters, then twelve. Scrooge's repeater beats twelve. He gropes to the frosted window, rubs a pane — still night, still fog. He lies awake. Quarters pass. At last:

(SOUND: deep dull hollow ONE)

Light flashes. Bed-curtains at his face are drawn aside by a hand. THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands close: childlike yet old, white hair, unwrinkled face with tender bloom, long muscular bare arms and legs, white tunic, lustrous belt, holly branch, dress trimmed with summer flowers; a bright jet of light from its head; great extinguisher-cap under its arm. The figure fluctuates — one arm, twenty legs, then whole again.

(MUSIC: high thin crystalline tone under the light)

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

SCROOGE
What business brings you here?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare! Your reclamation, then. Take heed! Rise! and walk with me!

It clasps his arm. Scrooge resists toward the window.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit touches his heart. They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD AND SCHOOL - DAY

Clear cold winter day, snow on the ground. Open road, fields, a little market-town in the distance. Boys on ponies shout Merry Christmas. Scrooge, nightcap and all, clasps his hands.

(SOUND: crisp snow underfoot, boys' shouts, distant church)

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

They approach a dull red-brick mansion of broken fortunes — damp offices, broken windows, fowls in the stables. Inside a long bare schoolroom, a lonely BOY SCROOGE reads near a feeble fire. Grown Scrooge sits on a form and weeps.

(SOUND: mice behind panelling, drip from a water-spout, empty door swing)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

SCROOGE
Poor boy! I wish—but it's too late now. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Ghost smiles, waves its hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

The boy grows larger. The room darker, dirtier. A little girl, FAN, darts in, arms about his neck.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Father is so much kinder than he used to be. And you're to be a man! And first, we're to be together all the Christmas long.

BOY SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

She drags him toward the door in childish eagerness.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. God forbid I should gainsay it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge looks uneasy.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

A warehouse made snug as a ball-room. OLD FEZZIWIG, Welsh wig, capacious waistcoat, jovial, claps from a high desk. Young Ebenezer and DICK WILKINS clear shutters and floor. A fiddler tunes. MRS. FEZZIWIG and three Miss Fezziwigs, employees, housemaid, baker, cook, milkman pour in. Dancing — twenty couple — Sir Roger de Coverley. Fezziwig cuts so deftly he seems to wink with his legs.

(SOUND: fiddle scrape and reel, laughter, hand-claps, fire roar)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Hilli-ho! Clear away, my lads!

At eleven, Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig shake every hand at the door with Merry Christmas. The apprentices' praise floats back. Scrooge's heart is in the scene.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

He stops under the Spirit's glance.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

EXT. CITY STREET / INT. BELLE'S PARTING PLACE - EVENING

Young man SCROOGE, prime of life, face beginning to harden with care and avarice, sits with BELLE in mourning-dress; tears in her eyes in the Ghost's light.

(SOUND: muffled street traffic, soft room hush)

BELLE
It matters little. Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Have I not?

YOUNG SCROOGE
Even if I have grown so much wiser, what then? I am not changed towards you.

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. You are changed. When it was made, you were another man. If this had never been between us, would you seek me out and try to win me now? Ah, no!

She releases him with a full heart, for the love of him he once was, and leaves. They part.

SCROOGE
Spirit! show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

INT. BELLE'S HOME - NIGHT

A comfortable room. Belle, now a comely matron, and her daughter, and a tumult of children. A husband enters with a man laden with Christmas toys. Joy, parcels, shouting. Later by the fire the husband mentions he saw Mr. Scrooge alone in his office — partner dying — quite alone in the world.

(SOUND: children's uproar, then fire crackle, quiet talk)

SCROOGE
Spirit! remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

He wrestles the Ghost. Light burns high from its head. Scrooge seizes the extinguisher-cap and presses it down. Light still streams from under it. Exhaustion; his own bedroom; heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes in a tough snore to the stroke of One. He parts all curtains himself. A blaze of ruddy light from the adjoining room. He shuffles to the door. A strange voice calls his name.

(SOUND: bell ONE, then deep silence, then the voice)

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - TRANSFORMED SITTING-ROOM - NIGHT

Walls and ceiling hung with living green, holly, mistletoe, ivy. Mighty blaze in the hearth. A throne of turkeys, geese, game, joints, sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, oysters, chestnuts, apples, oranges, bowls of punch. Upon it sits the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT — jolly Giant in green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast and feet, holly wreath with icicles, glowing torch like Plenty's horn, antique rusted scabbard without sword.

(SOUND: fire roar, punch seethe, berry-bright stillness)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me! You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Have never walked forth with the younger members of my family — my elder brothers born in these later years?

SCROOGE
I am afraid I have not. Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge holds fast. Room, fire, feast vanish.

EXT. LONDON STREETS - CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

Snow on roofs, furrowed mud and ice below. People shovel, laugh, fling snowballs. Poulterers' and fruiterers' radiant. Church bells. Poor people carry dinners to bakers. The Spirit sprinkles incense from his torch; angry jostlers soften.

(SOUND: scrapers on pavement, bells, market bustle, torch hiss)

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own. To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

They pass on, invisible, to the suburbs.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - DAY

Four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT, poor twice-turned gown brave in ribbons, lays the cloth with BELINDA. PETER plunges a fork into potatoes, monstrous borrowed collar at his mouth. Two smaller Cratchits scream of the goose. MARTHA arrives from the milliner's; they hide her for a joke as BOB CRATCHIT enters with TINY TIM upon his shoulder — little crutch, iron frame on his limbs, Bob's long comforter hanging down.

(SOUND: potato bubble, crutch tip on boards, children's shrieks of joy)

MRS. CRATCHIT
What has ever got your precious father then? And your brother, Tiny Tim!

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha runs from the closet into his arms. The young Cratchits bear Tiny Tim off to hear the pudding sing in the copper.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice trembles. Tiny Tim's crutch returns; he takes his stool by the fire. Goose procession, gravy, apple-sauce, mashed potatoes, grace. The goose is enough for all. Mrs. Cratchit brings the pudding like a speckled cannon-ball, blazing in ignited brandy, holly on top.

(SOUND: knife in goose, murmur of delight, brandy flare, spoon clink)

Later: cloth cleared, chestnuts on the fire, hot gin-and-lemon in two tumblers and a custard-cup without a handle. Family in a half-circle.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tim sits close. Bob holds his withered little hand.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head at his own words.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

Bob raises a toast.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon. Odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man!

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year!

The shadow of Scrooge's name passes. They grow merry again. Tiny Tim sings in a plaintive little voice. The Spirit's torch brightens them as they fade. Scrooge's eye stays on Tim until the last.

EXT. BLEAK MOOR / LIGHTHOUSE / SHIP - NIGHT

Montage under the Spirit's robe: miners' hut with old song and chorus; lighthouse keepers with grog and a sturdy song; shipboard men humming Christmas tunes at wheel and look-out.

(SOUND: wind on moor, sea thunder, rough chorus, ship's timber creak)

INT. FRED'S DRAWING-ROOM - NIGHT

Bright dry gleaming room. FRED laughs until he holds his sides — irresistible. FRED'S WIFE (the niece), pretty, dimpled, and friends roar with him, including TOPPER.

(SOUND: peals of laughter, fire, later harp and glee)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S WIFE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow. His offences carry their own punishment. I am sorry for him. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it if he finds me going there, year after year. If it only puts him in the vein to leave his poor clerk fifty pounds, that's something.

Games: blind-man's buff (Topper always finds the plump sister), forfeits, Yes and No. The answer is Uncle Scrooge. They drink his health anyway.

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge, unseen, would have pledged them. The Spirit will not let him stay.

EXT. OPEN PLACE - NIGHT

The Ghost has grown old; hair grey. Chimes ring three quarters past eleven.

(SOUND: chimes, cold wind)

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night. To-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am not justified in what I ask, but I see something strange protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

From the robe: two children, wretched, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish — a boy and girl — cling to its outside.

(SOUND: their thin breath, city murmur under Doom)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost is gone. A solemn PHANTOM, draped and hooded in deep black, only one outstretched hand visible, comes like a mist along the ground.

(SOUND: twelve strokes dying; then profound hush)

INT. CITY / EXCHANGE - DAY

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME points onward. Scrooge follows in the shadow of its dress among merchants who chink money and talk.

(SOUND: coin chink, boots on stone, indifferent voices)

FAT MERCHANT
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MERCHANT HALES
When did he die?

FAT MERCHANT
Last night, I believe.

MERCHANT GRAY
What was the matter with him? I thought he'd never die.

FAT MERCHANT
God knows. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. Upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

They laugh and stroll away. Elsewhere two wealthy men:

MR. CHAPMAN
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. PELL
So I am told. Cold, isn't it? Good morning!

No more. Scrooge looks for his own image in his usual corner — another man stands there.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG-AND-BOTTLE SHOP - NIGHT

Obscure foul street. Low shop of iron, rags, bottles, bones. OLD JOE by a charcoal stove. CHARWOMAN with a heavy bundle, MRS. DILBER the laundress, UNDERTAKER'S MAN in faded black — all three meet and laugh.

(SOUND: rusty hinge shriek, pipe puff, bundle thump)

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Look here, old Joe, here's a chance! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour.

Behind the rag screen they open plunder: seal, pencil-case, sleeve-buttons; sheets, towels, teaspoons, sugar-tongs; then bed-curtains, blankets, and the dead man's best shirt taken off him.

CHARWOMAN
Why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death, instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself.

MRS. DILBER
It's a judgment on him.

CHARWOMAN
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

Scrooge shudders from head to foot.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

Bare uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet, a body, plundered, unwatched, unwept. Pale light on it. A cat at the door; rats beneath the hearth-stone.

(SOUND: cat scratch, rat gnaw, terrible stillness)

The Phantom points to the head. Scrooge cannot lift the cover.

SCROOGE
Spirit! this is a fearful place. Let us go!

Still the finger points.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

INT. DEBTORS' HOME - DAY

A mother, CAROLINE, anxious, starts at every sound. Her careworn young husband enters. News: the merciless creditor is dead. Their hearts are lighter. The children's faces brighter. A happier house for this man's death.

(SOUND: clock tick, children's hushed play, relieved breath)

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOME - NIGHT

Quiet. Very quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and daughters sew. Peter reads. The noisy little Cratchits still as statues. Bob enters in his comforter, walks slower. Tea on the hob. He breaks down.

(SOUND: soft sewing, then Bob's stifled sob)

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

He goes upstairs to the Christmas-hung room, sits by the empty place, kisses the little face, comes down reconciled. He tells of Fred's kindness in the street. They promise never to forget Tiny Tim.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead!

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY

Walled in by houses, overrun by grass and weeds, choked with too much burying. The Spirit stands among the graves and points down to one. Scrooge advances trembling.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, far bell, Scrooge's shaking breath)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Ghost points downward still.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

Immovable. Scrooge creeps to the stone and reads: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

(SOUND: a single crow; then silence)

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from grave to him and back.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
Spirit! hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

The hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

The kind hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He clutches the spectral hand. The Spirit repulses him. Holding up his hands in a last prayer, he sees the Phantom shrink, collapse, and dwindle into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - MORNING

The bedpost is his own. The bed, the room, his own. Time before him his own, to make amends in.

(SOUND: church bells pealing lustiest clash and clang)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

He scrambles out, laughing and crying, tangled in stockings, folding a bed-curtain in his arms.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down, rings and all. They are here—I am here—the shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled. I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy!

He frisks into the sitting-room, winded, naming every corner where the Ghosts were.

SCROOGE
It's all right, it's all true, it all happened. Ha ha ha!

He runs to the window, opens it. No fog. Clear, bright, jovial cold. Golden sunlight. Merry bells.

(SOUND: glorious pealing bells, sweet fresh air rush)

SCROOGE
What's to-day!

STREET BOY
(below, in Sunday clothes)
Eh?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

STREET BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. Hallo, my fine fellow! Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner? The prize Turkey — not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

STREET BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck! Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot. Scrooge rubs his hands.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

He pats the knocker as he waits.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Merry Christmas!

EXT. LONDON STREETS - DAY

Scrooge dressed in his best, smiling at everyone. Good-humoured fellows wish him merry Christmas — the blithest sounds in his ears. He meets the Portly Gentleman, takes both his hands.

(SOUND: crowd murmur, footsteps, bright street life)

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. A merry Christmas to you, sir! Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it. Will you come and see me?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. Bless you!

He goes to church, pats children, questions beggars, finds pleasure in everything. In the afternoon he turns toward Fred's house, passes the door a dozen times, then knocks.

INT. FRED'S DINING-ROOM - DAY

Table in great array. Scrooge sidles his face round the door.

(SOUND: warm room hum, then astonished delight)

SCROOGE
Fred! It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred nearly shakes his arm off. Hearty welcome. Niece, Topper, plump sister, wonderful party, wonderful unanimity, wonderful happiness.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

Scrooge early, door wide open to the Tank. Clock strikes nine. No Bob. A quarter past. Bob, full eighteen minutes and a half late, hat and comforter off, on his stool in a jiffy, pen racing.

(SOUND: clock strike, frantic quill)

SCROOGE
(feigning the old growl)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He leaps from his stool, digs Bob in the waistcoat so Bob staggers back into the Tank — then claps him on the back.

(SOUND: stool scrape, friendly thump, Scrooge's earnest laugh)

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary! A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

EXT. LONDON / CRATCHIT DOORSTEP - DAY (MONTAGE)

Scrooge better than his word — infinitely more. Tiny Tim does NOT die; Scrooge is a second father. He becomes as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew. Some laugh at the alteration; he lets them. His own heart laughs.

(MUSIC: warm swelling carol theme)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge.

Tiny Tim, strong on his crutch beside Scrooge and Bob, looks out at the bright street.

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END
